// Build spec §4's own worked example, verbatim in shape: a user in one
// organization must never get `view` on a document belonging to another.
// `distinct(orgA, orgB)` is the entire reason this needs to be a
// constraint problem rather than plain reachability — "cross-tenant"
// means precisely that these two variables must bind to different
// objects, and a reachability answer that quietly let them collapse onto
// the same node would be a false negative no amount of testing catches.
//
// `tenant` is not a first-class schema concept anywhere in this project —
// it's an ordinary relation from a subject/object to the `organization`
// that scopes it, same as any other relation. This invariant says that
// without the invariant language itself needing to know what a "tenant"
// is; §5 resolves `tenant` against whatever real schema it's checked
// against.
//
// Expected verdict once §5 exists, against
// tools/schema-verifier/fixtures/schemas/tenancy.authz: VIOLATED, not
// HOLDS — and that's the real, useful finding, not a fixture bug. That
// schema's `user.tenant` relation and `document.view = tenant->member`
// are two independent relations; `tenant(s) = orgA` pins s's *own*
// declared tenant, but `document.view`'s actual permission logic never
// once consults `user.tenant` — it only asks whether s is a `member` of
// whatever org `o`'s own `tenant` tuple names. Nothing stops a witness
// from giving s a *separate*, unconstrained `organization:orgB#member`
// tuple alongside its `tenant(s) = orgA` fact — the two are unrelated
// claims about s, and the search finds exactly that witness. This is the
// verifier doing its job: a schema author who wrote `tenant(s) = orgA`
// expecting it to gate `document.view` would be wrong, and this is the
// proof. See docs/DECISIONS.md D-116 for the general principle (a
// relation-equality constraint can prove a leak real; it essentially
// never proves one impossible) and why HOLDS instead needs type-level
// unreachability — see no-public-path-to-private-document.invariant.
invariant tenant_isolation {
  s: user
  o: document
  orgA: organization
  orgB: organization

  distinct(orgA, orgB)
  tenant(s) = orgA
  tenant(o) = orgB

  goal: view(s, o)
}
